Docker Quick Summary

1. Docker
A tool that packages your application along with all its dependencies and environment so it runs the same everywhere.
Problem it solves:
    "It works on my machine but not on yours."


2. Dockerfile

A text file containing instructions for building your application environment.
Think:
    Recipe
Example tasks:
    Use Python
    Install libraries
    Copy code
    Start application


3. Docker Image
The packaged result created from a Dockerfile.
Think:
Recipe → Packaged Meal Kit
Contains:
    Code
    Python/Java runtime
    Libraries
    Configurations
    Not running yet.


4. Docker Container
A running instance of an image.
Think:
    Meal Kit → Cooked Food
or
    Class → Object
    Image → Container
One image can create many containers.


5. Docker Engine
The actual software that manages everything.
Responsibilities:
    Build images
    Run containers
    Stop containers
    Delete containers
Think:
    Engine = Factory Manager


6. Docker CLI
Commands you type.
Examples:
    docker build
    docker run
    docker stop
Think:
    CLI = Remote Control
    Engine = Machine


7. Docker Hub
Online repository of ready-made images.
Think:
    GitHub for Docker Images
Contains images for:
    Python
    MySQL
    PostgreSQL
    MongoDB
    Redis
    Ubuntu


8. Volumes
Permanent storage for containers.
Without volume:
    Container Deleted
    ↓
    Data Lost
With volume:
    Container Deleted
    ↓
    Data Safe
Think:
    Container = Hotel Room
    Volume = Bank Locker


9. Networks
Allow containers to communicate with each other.
Example:
Frontend
   ↓
Backend
   ↓
Database
Without networking, they cannot talk.


10. Docker Compose
Manages multiple containers together.
Without Compose:
    Start Frontend
    Start Backend
    Start Database
    Start Redis
...
With Compose:
    docker compose up
    Everything starts together.
Think:
    Docker = One Container
    Compose = Complete Application


11. Kubernetes
Manages hundreds or thousands of containers in production.
Handles:
    Auto restart
    Auto scaling
    Load balancing
    Deployment
Think:
    Docker = One Restaurant
    Compose = One Restaurant Building
    Kubernetes = Entire Restaurant Chain